    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p5-q14-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Let $a$ and $b$ be rational numbers with $a<b$. Prove that there is some irrational number that lies between $a$ and $b$. Hint: Show that $c=a+\\frac{b-a}{\\sqrt2}$ is an irrational number that lies between $a$ and $b$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\displaystyle c=a+\\frac{b-a}{\\sqrt2}$ is irrational and $a<c<b$.\n\nIf $c$ were rational, rearranging would make $\\sqrt2$ rational, a contradiction.",
          "worked": "Set $c=a+\\frac{b-a}{\\sqrt2}$. Since $b-a>0$ and $0<\\frac1{\\sqrt2}<1$,\n$$0<\\frac{b-a}{\\sqrt2}<b-a.$$\nAdding $a$ gives $a<c<b$.\n\nSuppose $c$ were rational. Rearranging gives\n$$\\sqrt2=\\frac{b-a}{c-a}.$$\nThe numerator and the nonzero denominator would both be rational, so $\\sqrt2$ would be rational. This contradicts its proven irrationality. Hence $c$ is irrational and lies between $a$ and $b$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 70
      },
      {
        "id": "p5-q14-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Hence, prove that there are infinitely many irrational numbers between every pair of distinct rational numbers.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "There are infinitely many irrational numbers between any two distinct rational numbers.\n\nApply part (a) to infinitely many disjoint intervals with rational endpoints inside the original interval.",
          "worked": "Let $a<b$ be rational. Define rational numbers\n$$r_k=a+\\frac{b-a}{2^k}\\qquad(k=0,1,2,\\ldots).$$\nThen $r_0=b$ and $a<r_{k+1}<r_k\\le b$. By part (a), each interval $(r_{k+1},r_k)$ contains an irrational number.\n\nThese intervals are disjoint, so the irrational numbers chosen from them are distinct. There are infinitely many such intervals, all inside $(a,b)$. Therefore infinitely many irrational numbers lie between $a$ and $b$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 60
      }
    ]
  },
